.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_ALLOCATED_BYTES 3 "2026-09-16" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes \- report the memory a pool holds for its entries
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the size in bytes of the single block mp holds for its entries
and their per-entry state.

This is what the pool costs, which ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3)
deliberately does not report. A thread-safe pool large enough to keep
per-thread caches holds a reserve beyond the count it was created with,
so its memory exceeds what its usable capacity accounts for. Use this
call to size a pool against a memory budget rather than reproducing the
reserve arithmetic.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the size in bytes of the pool's entry block
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The entry block is counted and nothing else. The handle itself is a
small fixed allocation; per-thread caches are separate allocations made
lazily as threads first use the pool, bounded by the same reserve; and
entries served by the dynamic fallback are not part of this block at all,
so ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(3) is what accounts for those.

For a pool built by
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(3) this reports the part of
the caller's own buffer that was divided into entries. Such a pool never
receives a cache and carries no reserve, so the figure is exactly the
element count times the stride, plus one status byte per element. The
pool allocated none of it.

The value does not change over the pool's lifetime.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Acquires the pool's lock, so it is a cold-path call and not intended for
use inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_used_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
